## What I changed

The old page was a stack of undifferentiated grey boxes — every element had the same visual weight, so the three things a patient actually needs were buried among the things they don't.

**Status is now computed, not hardcoded.** The old page said "Open now for walk-ins. Last patient accepted at 19:30" as static text — which is wrong six days a week and dangerously wrong on Sunday. `readStatus()` (`src/App.jsx:110`) reads the real clock against the hours table and produces one of five honest states: open now, closing soon (registration shut but come in if urgent), not open yet, closed for today, closed today — each with the correct next-opening time. It refreshes every minute for pages left open in a waiting room. Right now, on a Sunday, the page correctly says *"We are closed today. We open again tomorrow at 8:00."*

**Visual hierarchy matched to the actual questions:**
- *Can I be seen?* — a single large status card is the first thing below the masthead, colour-coded green/amber with a left rule, carrying the badge, the plain-language headline, and the last-intake time.
- *What does it cost?* — the three assurances went from small bordered chips to full cards with checkmarks and a plain-language second line ("Bring nothing. We will never ask for a card."). Every service card leads with a green cost pill before its eligibility line, so scanning the section reads as a column of "Free".
- *How do I get there?* — the address and phone are a dark teal card at display size, transit is an icon list with the mode bolded out front, and a fixed bottom call bar keeps Call/Directions one tap away on mobile at all scroll positions.

**Other judgement calls:** the emergency notice is a full-bleed red band above everything with `call 911` as a live `tel:` link, not a pale pink box mid-page. Today's row is highlighted in the hours table with a "Today" tag. The booking form is de-emphasised and paired with a panel saying most people don't need it, since the old page's prominent form invited patients to wait for a callback when they could just walk in. The interpreter line got promoted from footnote text to a real call button.

I preserved every required element verbatim — the six services with their exact eligibility and cost strings, all seven language entries, both phone numbers, the transit lines, the late-arrival note, and the existing `id`/`data-day`/`data-service` hooks.
